title: The session scenario
tags: Development
kind: reference
audience: dev
aliases: session.sh session
summary: scripts/session.sh drives a real GUI through a day's worth of use on a 50 000-file repository, each step against its own budget, with the slow log at its floor saying where the time and the keys went — a report, run on purpose.

```
scripts/session.sh --unsandboxed           # the whole session (a container: no WebKit sandbox)
scripts/session.sh --data benchmarks/bench_data   # the 5 000-file folder, for a fast run
scripts/session.sh --keep                  # keep target/bench-data/session: logs, report, repository
scripts/session.sh --calibrate             # measure the machine's neutral load (30 s)
scripts/session.sh --record                # append the run to benchmarks/session/<machine>.jsonl
```

[[The timed regression suite]] times a list of daemon calls someone wrote down. What
it cannot find is what nobody wrote down: the request a panel sends that it should
not, the read that waits two seconds for a lock while the user does something else,
the polling between two actions. Those were found by reading the [[Slow log]] of a
repository in daily use — and a slow log that has to wait for daily use is neither
reproducible nor fast. The session is that use, scripted.

!! What a run does

`metafolder-bench session` builds a fresh repository on a hard-linked copy of
`benchmarks/bench_data_big` (50 000 files) under `target/bench-data/session/`,
starts a daemon of its own and a real GUI on it, and runs a fixed list of steps:
the first reconcile, the metarecord list (open, five pages, the finder, a typed
query), the detail panel following the list, the file manager down to the largest
folder, the tree explorer, a bulk write on the list's query with its undo and redo,
the log panel, the filesystem changing under the watcher (a folder renamed, a
thousand files created then deleted), the duplicate scan, the orphan scan, a
reconcile with nothing to do, ten seconds of nothing, and the repository unloaded and
loaded again.

The GUI steps go through the scripting API (`/gui/command`, `/gui/panels`), as
[[Testing the GUI end to end]] does. A step is over when the user would stop
waiting: when its command resolved and the panels stopped recording measures
(`/gui/bench`) for 300 ms — the time of the last measure, not of the silence. A
filesystem step is over when a query sees the change.

Determinism comes from the inputs: the copy is rebuilt every run, the GUI reads a
configuration built from the shipped defaults (`crates/*/default-config/`) and never
the user's, the daemon one with no automatic backup. Only the durations vary with
the machine.

!! The report

Each step has a ''budget'': what a user should wait for it on a repository of that
size — a product judgment written next to the step in `crates/bench/src/session/mod.rs`,
not what it takes today. Some have a ''key budget'' too, where a bound is part of the
design (a page of the list, an idle panel); keys do not depend on the machine.

The daemon and the GUI run with every slow-log threshold at its floor (1 ms, one
key), so ''every'' operation is logged with its phases. An entry belongs to the
step whose window holds its start; one that started between steps is listed on its
own. The table gives each step's wall time, budget, the keys the daemon read, the
operations and the slowest of them; every step that is not `ok` — `SLOW`, `READS`,
`FAILED` — is followed by its three slowest operations, phase by phase. A step that
failed stops the session: what would follow would measure another state.

The exit status says whether every step ran, not whether they were fast: this is a
report. The work directory goes at the end unless `--keep`.

!! Recorded runs and the neutral load

A duration measured on a busy machine says little. A run worth keeping is taken on a
machine left ''neutral'' — its user running nothing heavy — and that state is
measured once: `--calibrate` samples the CPU use of the whole machine for 30 s
(`/proc/stat`, the host's even from a container) and the load average, into
`benchmarks/session/<machine>.neutral.json`. `--record` samples it again for 10 s
before the session and after, refuses to record (exit 2) when the machine is more
than 5 points busier than neutral (`--force` records anyway), and appends the run —
commit, loads, each step's time, keys and operations — to
`benchmarks/session/<machine>.jsonl`.

Claude does not start a recorded run, nor a calibration, without asking the user
first: the user is the one who has to keep the machine quiet meanwhile.

!! Limits

* It never writes into an existing file: a hard link shares its bytes with the original folder.
* It needs a display and a built frontend, so it is not part of `scripts/check.sh`.
